# The World Wars and the Cold War: 1914-1991

> Two world wars killed some eighty million people and ended European
> dominance; the Cold War that followed divided the planet between two
> superpowers for four decades. The chain runs: alliance systems,
> Versailles, depression and dictators, blitzkrieg and the bomb, the UN,
> and the wall that came down in 1989.

## 1. The Causes of World War I

Historians summarise the deep causes as **MAIN** — Militarism, Alliances,
Imperialism, Nationalism: the Anglo-German naval race, rigid blocs,
colonial rivalries, and Balkan Slav ambitions against Austria-Hungary.

@diagram tree id="wwi-alliances" layout="tree" aria="The two alliance blocs of World War One"
  WWI Alliances -> "Central Powers"
  WWI Alliances -> "Triple Entente"
  Central Powers -> "Germany"
  Central Powers -> "Austria-Hungary"
  Central Powers -> "Ottoman Empire, Bulgaria"
  Triple Entente -> "Britain and its empire"
  Triple Entente -> "France"
  Triple Entente -> "Russia, later Italy, Japan, USA"
@/diagram

The spark: at **Sarajevo on 28 June 1914**, **Gavrilo Princip** of the
Black Hand assassinated Archduke **Franz Ferdinand**. Austria's ultimatum,
Russia's mobilisation, and the alliance cascade produced general war by
early August.

## 2. The Course of the War

Germany's Schlieffen Plan stalled at the **Marne (1914)**; the Western
Front froze into **trench warfare** from Flanders to Switzerland, where
**Verdun and the Somme (1916)** each cost hundreds of thousands of lives.
Unrestricted U-boat warfare (the *Lusitania*, 1915) and the **Zimmermann
telegram** brought the **United States in (April 1917)**; revolutionary
Russia left at **Brest-Litovsk (1918)**. Germany's 1918 offensive failed,
and the armistice came on **11 November 1918**.

## 3. The Versailles Settlement

The **Treaty of Versailles (28 June 1919)**: **Article 231** assigned sole
**war guilt** to Germany; reparations were fixed in 1921 at 132 billion
gold marks; Germany lost territory, fleet, and colonies, its army capped
at 100,000. The **League of Nations** was born at Geneva — without the
United States, with unanimity rules and no army, powerless before Japan in
**Manchuria (1931)** and Italy in **Ethiopia (1935)**.

[warning]
The settlement punished without reconciling: German resentment, Italian
disappointment at a "mutilated victory," and Japanese exclusions fed the
next war within a generation.
[/warning]

## 4. The Interwar Years: Depression and Dictators

The **Wall Street Crash (October 1929)** became the **Great Depression** —
a quarter of America's workforce jobless, world trade and banks
collapsing. Economic despair incubated ideology: **Mussolini's** Fascists
marched on **Rome (October 1922)**; **Hitler** became Chancellor on
**30 January 1933**, and the Reichstag fire plus Enabling Act made him
dictator within months, the Nuremberg Laws (1935) legalising persecution.
The democracies appeased — Rhineland (1936), Anschluss and the Sudetenland
at **Munich (1938)**, Chamberlain's "peace for our time" — until the
**Nazi-Soviet Pact (23 August 1939)** cleared the way for war.

## 5. World War II: Course and Turning Points

Germany's **blitzkrieg** destroyed Poland from **1 September 1939**; France
fell in June 1940; the **Battle of Britain** (1940) stopped invasion in
the air; Hitler turned east (**Barbarossa, June 1941**) and Japan struck
**Pearl Harbor (7 December 1941)**, globalising the war.

| Turning point | Date | Effect |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Midway | June 1942 | Japanese naval advance broken |
| El Alamein | Oct-Nov 1942 | Axis pushed out of Egypt |
| Stalingrad | Feb 1943 | German Sixth Army destroyed |
| D-Day, Normandy | 6 June 1944 | Second front opened |
| Berlin falls | May 1945 | Germany surrenders 8 May |

## 6. The Holocaust and the Atomic End

The Nazi genocide murdered **six million Jews** — the **Holocaust** — in
death camps such as **Auschwitz**, coordinated at the Wannsee Conference
(1942). President **Truman** authorised atomic bombs on **Hiroshima
(6 August 1945)** and **Nagasaki (9 August)**; Japan surrendered on
15 August (instruments signed 2 September). The war's dead exceeded sixty
million.

## 7. The United Nations

Founded by the **San Francisco Charter, 24 October 1945** (51 original
members), the **UN** replaced the League with six organs — General
Assembly, **Security Council**, ECOSOC, Trusteeship Council, the
International Court of Justice, and Secretariat — under five veto-wielding
permanent members: the USA, USSR (now Russia), Britain, France, and China.

## 8. The Cold War: Containment to Collapse

- **1947:** the **Truman Doctrine** (aid to Greece and Turkey) and
  **Marshall Plan** made containment of communism official policy.
- **1948-49:** the **Berlin blockade** was defeated by an Allied airlift;
  Germany split into two states.
- **1949-55:** **NATO** founded, answered by the **Warsaw Pact**.
- **1950-53:** the **Korean War** froze at the 38th parallel; the
  **Vietnam War** (to 1975) later bled America.
- **1962:** the **Cuban Missile Crisis** — thirteen days at the nuclear
  brink — taught both sides caution.
- **1961:** Nehru, Nasser, and Tito founded the **Non-Aligned Movement**
  at Belgrade, refusing both blocs.
- **1970s detente:** SALT I and Helsinki, shattered by the 1979 Soviet
  invasion of Afghanistan.
- **1985-1991:** Gorbachev's **glasnost** and **perestroika** loosened the
  system; the **Berlin Wall fell (9 November 1989)**, Germany reunified
  (1990), and the **USSR dissolved (December 1991)**.

| Era | Years | Character |
| --- | --- | --- |
| World War I | 1914-18 | Industrial trench war; empires fall |
| Interwar | 1919-39 | Versailles order, depression, dictators |
| World War II | 1939-45 | Total war, Holocaust, atomic bomb |
| Cold War | 1947-89 | Bipolar rivalry without direct war |
| After | 1989-91 | Wall falls; USSR dissolves |

[note]
In one line: the wars of 1914-45 exhausted Europe; the Cold War split it;
1989-91 reunited it. The UN, nuclear deterrence, and decolonisation —
India's 1947 independence among them — define the world since.
[/note]

## Key Takeaways

- MAIN causes plus the Sarajevo spark produced World War I; Versailles'
  war guilt and League weakness invited round two.
- The Depression birthed fascist dictators; appeasement delayed and
  enlarged the war until blitzkrieg and Pearl Harbor globalised it.
- Stalingrad, Midway, El Alamein, and D-Day turned the tide; the Holocaust
  and Hiroshima-Nagasaki redefined war's moral and physical limits.
- The UN (1945) and the Cold War's containment, crises, and detente
  structured world politics until 1989-91 closed the short twentieth
  century.
